::ENG2_U01_UA_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The school added a homework help table in the cafeteria before first period. Students could ask quick questions before class began. Question: Which choice best paraphrases the passage?{
=A. The help table gave students a place to ask homework questions before class.#Correct. This accurately restates the main idea.
~B. The cafeteria stopped serving breakfast before first period.#Incorrect. The passage does not mention breakfast service.
~C. Students could not ask questions before class.#Incorrect. The passage says students could ask quick questions.
~D. The homework table was used after school only.#Incorrect. The passage says before first period.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The teacher said the second response was more precise because it named the exact evidence. Question: What does precise mean?{
~A. unclear#Incorrect. Precise means clear and exact.
~B. hidden#Incorrect. Precise information is not hidden.
~C. unrelated#Incorrect. Precise information should relate to the idea.
=D. exact and clear#Correct. Precise means exact and clear.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The library added signs showing fiction, nonfiction, and graphic novel sections. New students used the signs to locate books without asking for directions. Question: Which evidence best supports "The signs helped students find books independently"?{
~A. The library had fiction.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~B. Graphic novels were listed.#Incorrect. This is only one category.
=C. New students used the signs to locate books without asking for directions.#Correct. This directly supports the paraphrase.
~D. The signs were in the library.#Incorrect. This gives location but not the result.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Passage: The school posted bus updates on a screen near the office. Students checked the screen before leaving. Question: Which response is written correctly?{
~A. the screen helped students check bus updates before leaving#Incorrect. This needs capitalization and punctuation.
=B. The screen helped students check bus updates before leaving.#Correct. This sentence is complete and correctly punctuated.
~C. The screen helped students it had updates.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~D. Because students checked the screen before leaving.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The cafeteria labeled vegetarian meals on the menu. Students could identify those choices quickly. Question: Which paraphrase is most accurate?{
=A. Menu labels helped students quickly recognize vegetarian meal options.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and uses new wording.
~B. Students removed vegetarian meals from the menu.#Incorrect. The passage says meals were labeled.
~C. The cafeteria stopped serving lunch.#Incorrect. The passage does not say lunch stopped.
~D. Students could not find vegetarian choices.#Incorrect. The labels helped them identify choices.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The article gives facts from a school survey to support the principal's decision. Question: Which academic word best describes the facts from the survey?{
~A. rumor#Incorrect. Survey facts are not rumors.
~B. guess#Incorrect. Survey facts are collected information, not guesses.
~C. decoration#Incorrect. Facts are not decoration.
=D. evidence#Correct. Evidence is information that supports an idea.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The teacher placed vocabulary cards beside the reading passage. Students used the cards to understand difficult words before answering questions. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The cards were beside something and questions happened.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Students answered questions before using the cards.#Incorrect. The passage says they used cards before answering.
=C. Vocabulary cards helped students understand difficult words before answering questions.#Correct. This accurately paraphrases the passage.
~D. The teacher removed the reading passage.#Incorrect. The passage stayed in use.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Passage: The teacher gave students a checklist before they submitted paragraphs. The checklist reminded them to check evidence and punctuation. Question: Which revision is best?{
~A. The checklist helped students revise paragraphs, it reminded them to check evidence and punctuation.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
=B. The checklist helped students revise paragraphs because it reminded them to check evidence and punctuation.#Correct. This is complete and clearly connected.
~C. the checklist helped students revise paragraphs#Incorrect. This needs capitalization and punctuation.
~D. Because it reminded them to check evidence and punctuation.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: A student says aloud, "The two texts contrast in mood because one feels hopeful and the other feels serious." Question: What does contrast mean?{
=A. show differences#Correct. The sentence shows different moods.
~B. copy exactly#Incorrect. Contrast does not mean copy.
~C. agree completely#Incorrect. The moods differ.
~D. hide meaning#Incorrect. Contrast reveals a difference.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The city painted crosswalks in brighter colors near the elementary school. Drivers could see the crossing areas more clearly during morning traffic. Question: Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. The city removed crosswalks near the school.#Incorrect. The city painted crosswalks.
~B. The school painted all drivers.#Incorrect. This is not logical.
~C. Drivers could not see morning traffic.#Incorrect. The passage says they could see crossing areas more clearly.
=D. Brighter crosswalk paint helped drivers see crossing areas more clearly.#Correct. This restates the main idea accurately.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The response says, "The chart organized club deadlines and helped students prioritize forms." Question: Which word means decide what should be done first?{
~A. organized#Incorrect. Organized means arranged clearly.
~B. forms#Incorrect. Forms are documents.
=C. prioritize#Correct. Prioritize means decide what should come first.
~D. chart#Incorrect. Chart is the tool, not the action.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The science class used a clear jar to model erosion. When water was poured over soil, students watched pieces of dirt move away. Question: Which evidence best supports "The model showed water moving soil"?{
~A. The class was a science class.#Incorrect. This is too general.
=B. Students watched pieces of dirt move away.#Correct. This directly supports the paraphrase.
~C. The jar was clear.#Incorrect. This explains visibility but not movement.
~D. Water was used.#Incorrect. This is not as complete as the movement detail.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Passage: The counselor sent deadline reminders to students. The reminders helped students submit forms on time. Question: Which sentence fixes the run-on "The reminders helped students they submitted forms on time"?{
~A. The reminders helped students, they submitted forms on time.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~B. Because the reminders helped students submit forms on time.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=C. The reminders helped students submit forms on time.#Correct. This fixes the run-on and keeps the meaning.
~D. reminders helped students submit forms on time#Incorrect. This needs capitalization and punctuation.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The teacher asked students to summarize the article by stating the main idea and two important details. Question: What does summarize mean?{
=A. state the main ideas briefly#Correct. This is the meaning of summarize.
~B. copy every word#Incorrect. Summarizing uses fewer words.
~C. add unrelated opinions#Incorrect. A summary stays connected to the text.
~D. erase the main idea#Incorrect. A summary includes the main idea.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The school created a quiet study room beside the media center. Students could use the room during lunch to finish homework or read without hallway noise. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The room helped because it was good.#Incorrect. This is vague and lacks evidence.
=B. The quiet study room helped students work during lunch because they could finish homework or read without hallway noise.#Correct. This integrates paraphrase and evidence.
~C. The hallway was noisy because students did homework.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~D. Students read because the media center was beside something.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The source is credible because it comes from school survey results. Question: What does credible mean?{
~A. confusing#Incorrect. Credible does not mean confusing.
~B. unrelated#Incorrect. Credible information can be relevant.
~C. decorative#Incorrect. Credible is about trust, not appearance.
=D. trustworthy#Correct. Credible means trustworthy or believable.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Passage: The community garden labeled plant rows. Visitors knew which vegetables were growing. Question: Which sentence is written correctly?{
~A. the labels helped visitors identify which vegetables were growing#Incorrect. This needs capitalization and punctuation.
~B. Because visitors knew which vegetables were growing.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=C. The labels helped visitors identify which vegetables were growing.#Correct. This is complete and correctly punctuated.
~D. The labels helped visitors they knew vegetables.#Incorrect. This is a run-on and unclear.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The school website added a translation button for family announcements. Families could read important updates in the language they understood best. Question: Which paraphrase is accurate?{
=A. The translation button helped families understand announcements in their best language.#Correct. This accurately restates the main idea.
~B. The website removed family announcements.#Incorrect. The website added a translation button.
~C. Families stopped reading updates because of the button.#Incorrect. The button helped them read updates.
~D. The language understood the website.#Incorrect. This is not logical.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The response is relevant because every sentence answers the question about the passage. Question: What does relevant mean?{
~A. hidden#Incorrect. Relevant does not mean hidden.
=B. connected to the topic#Correct. Relevant means connected to the topic or question.
~C. unrelated#Incorrect. Relevant is the opposite of unrelated.
~D. too long#Incorrect. Length does not define relevance.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The teacher highlighted key dates on the project calendar. Students could see when research notes, drafts, and final copies were due. Question: Which response best uses the passage?{
~A. The calendar was highlighted because projects existed.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Students finished final copies before research notes.#Incorrect. The passage does not say this.
~C. The teacher removed due dates from the calendar.#Incorrect. The teacher highlighted dates.
=D. Highlighted dates helped students track when each part of the project was due.#Correct. This paraphrases the benefit and uses evidence.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Passage: The teacher highlighted key dates on the project calendar. Students knew when notes, drafts, and final copies were due. Question: Which sentence uses commas correctly?{
~A. Students knew when notes drafts and final copies were due.#Incorrect. The list needs commas.
~B. Students knew, when notes, drafts, and final copies were due.#Incorrect. The comma after knew is unnecessary.
=C. Students knew when notes, drafts, and final copies were due.#Correct. The commas separate items in the list.
~D. Students knew when, notes drafts and final copies were due.#Incorrect. The comma after when is incorrect.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The graph indicates that attendance increased after reminders were posted. Question: What does indicates mean?{
=A. shows#Correct. The graph shows the attendance change.
~B. hides#Incorrect. Indicates means shows or points to information.
~C. interrupts#Incorrect. Indicates is not about interrupting.
~D. forgets#Incorrect. A graph does not forget.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The debate coach asked students to underline the sentence that best supported each claim. This helped speakers avoid using details that sounded interesting but did not prove their point. Question: Which paraphrase is most accurate?{
~A. The coach wanted students to use any interesting detail.#Incorrect. The passage says some interesting details may not prove the point.
=B. The coach wanted students to choose evidence that directly supported their claims.#Correct. This accurately restates the purpose.
~C. The coach stopped students from using claims.#Incorrect. Students still used claims.
~D. Speakers avoided all details.#Incorrect. They avoided details that did not prove the point.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The organized chart helped readers understand the schedule. Question: Which word is academic vocabulary?{
~A. helped#Incorrect. Helped is common, not the strongest academic word here.
~B. readers#Incorrect. Readers names people.
~C. schedule#Incorrect. Schedule is the topic word here.
=D. organized#Correct. Organized is academic vocabulary about arranging information clearly.
}

::ENG2_U01_UA_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.R.10; MLA.ENG2.V.01] Passage: The response says, "The checklist improved student writing because it reminded students to check evidence, punctuation, and sentence clarity." Question: Which Unit 1 skills are used in this response?{
~A. Only spelling#Incorrect. The response includes more than spelling.
=B. Paraphrase, academic vocabulary, evidence, and conventions#Correct. The response combines the major Unit 1 skills.
~C. Only oral presentation#Incorrect. The response is written and evidence-based.
~D. Only unrelated details#Incorrect. The details are relevant.
}
